==============================================================================
PANEL 2 - persistence (does window A predict window B?)
==============================================================================
  traders with both windows >= 25 decisions: 251
  Spearman rho(z_A, z_B) = -0.0758   p = 0.2316
  Pearson  r             = -0.0839

   decile(A)    n   mean z_A   mean z_B  median z_B
         1     25    -0.3350    -0.0060     -0.0066
         2     25    -0.1882    -0.0492     -0.0557
         3     25    -0.1302    -0.0935     -0.0838
         4     25    -0.0810    -0.0913     -0.0620
         5     25    -0.0373    -0.0468     -0.0763
         6     25     0.0039    -0.0091      0.0238
         7     25     0.0471    -0.0236     -0.0074
         8     25     0.1024    -0.0116      0.0197
         9     25     0.1710    -0.1925     -0.1043
        10     26     0.3117    -0.0955     -0.0985

  bottom decile in A -> mean z_B -0.0060
  top    decile in A -> mean z_B -0.0955
  separation (top - bottom) = -0.0894
  The thesis needs SEPARATION, not just a negative bottom decile: in a
  population that is negative everywhere, 'bottom decile is negative' is
  satisfied by noise. Selection only works if bad-in-A stays worse in B.

